FS-03 of the Floor Studio series.

"96% match" with nothing under it is worthless. It is a number a
homeowner cannot check, cannot argue with and cannot repeat to their
partner, which means it decorates a five-figure decision rather than
moving one.

So lib/floor-studio/match.ts inverts the usual order. The score is not
computed and then explained. Every criterion returns a SENTENCE and a
signed weight; the score is the arithmetic over those weights; and
recomputeScore() reconstructs the number from the sentences. A test
asserts the two agree for the headline matches of every feel. A criterion
that cannot say why it moved the number is structurally unable to move it.

WHY NOT A MODEL

Nothing here a model would do better and four things it would do worse.
The result set must be restricted to configurations this company can
install, must be stable across a refresh, must be explainable to somebody
spending five figures, and must never invent a product. A scoring function
satisfies all four by construction, runs in the browser in under a
millisecond, and has no key, no vendor and no outage. AI earns its place
in this product at the room-reading step and in EcowoodsGuide. Here it
would be a costume.

AN EVIDENCE-FREE FLOOR SCORES 50, NOT 4

With no feels chosen, no photo and no room type, no criterion applies and
nothing is known either way. A recommender that prints 4% on a perfectly
good floor because the visitor has not answered anything yet is lying
about its own certainty. Only criteria that APPLY are counted toward the
denominator.

THREE WOODS, NOT ONE WOOD IN THREE HATS

Left alone the ranking returns three white oaks that differ by a finish.
That scores higher and is a worse answer: the visitor came to choose and
has been shown one choice wearing three hats. matchFloors caps the
headline set at one configuration per species, and ties break on the
configuration id so a refresh never reshuffles.

Budget never filters. Somebody who says 20k and falls in love with a 24k
floor is a conversation, not a hidden result — the floor is shown, with
the sentence saying it sits above their number.

21 tests.

